## Field semantics

| Field | Fill |
|---|---|
| REPO | label, not a path; or `none`. Repo-relative paths go inside the prompt |
| NEXT / CURRENT STAGE | receiving stage from Infer STAGE; NEXT is the short summary (bolded in header: `NEXT: **<stage>**`) |
| ADVISORS | human quick status of ADVICE. One question per ADVICE line: an advisor question with its follow-ups and retries, or one discussion. Write exactly one form: `none`; `N question(s), complete`; `N question(s): ` followed by only nonzero buckets in order complete, partial, failed, each partial/failed with a short parenthesized reason; or `unverified (<reason>); see ADVICE` when earlier advice cannot be reconstructed. Never write zero buckets or a trailing `none` |
| STATE | one-line working/commit/deployment/blocker summary |
| EXPOSURE | exposure of the next concrete action: Local, Shared or Consequential; name the actual boundary, not a sensitive topic |
| SHAPE | task shape: Micro, Bounded, Standard or Deep; state the evidence that makes the low route checkable or requires more capability |
| OWNERSHIP | `surgical` by default. `granted: <area>` only when the plan or the user asks for restructuring there (a refactor, or a feature in an area the plan says to reshape). REPO CONSTRAINTS then opens with: "Inside <area> this codebase is yours: how it's built is your call, and you'll keep building on it. List every change the task did not ask for. This relaxes surgical scope only: product decisions and stop boundaries still apply." Fix edits only the named failure whatever the value. Carry the value unchanged through Fix and into Verify Implementation, which checks those extra changes for correctness and regressions and does not reject them as scope |
| PLAN FILE | repo-relative, or `not needed: simple task` |
| SPEC FILES | spec/design/requirements in-repo or from the user prompt; `none known` if absent — never omit the field |
| RUN | one selected executable model + effort: `RUN: **<model>**, **<effort>**`. Choose through `routing`.{{if .Both}} Sufficiently supported comparable options use the stated tie-break; do not ask merely because there is no unique winner.{{end}} If no suitable executable route exists, write `RUN: blocked` and explain; never silently weaken the task |
{{if .Both}}| CLAUDE / GPT | both fixed provider lines, in alphabetical order, each with its best task-fit model and effort or an explicit unavailable reason. Task fit: suitable, conditional (name the guard), below need (name the gap), or insufficient evidence (name the missing calibration). Availability is separate: a reported usage limit or a model missing from the client or account can coexist with model capability. Bold model and effort only on eligible options. Never fill a line with an inadequate substitute or imply exact benchmark equality across snapshots; use the `routing` stage, worker and advisor rows. Effort names do not compare across models: match task capability on the `routing` capability ladder (Artificial Analysis index), and for coding stages also on FrontierCode 2.0 and Terminal-Bench 4.0. Where a stage-table row names a different alternative, the row wins |
{{end}}| ESCALATE | explicit failure or uncertainty trigger → next suitable route(s), with model and effort; or `none above`. A route, not permission to launch extra children.{{if .Codex}} Preserve Astra's gate and its high ceiling.{{end}} Escalation stays in the human header; carry task acceptance and stopping conditions into the receiving prompt |
| REQUIRED REVIEW | only reviews the user explicitly required (named model{{if .Both}}, cross-provider{{end}}, independence, coverage, or timing such as "before push"), kept close to the user's words. Each carries `met by <ADVICE line>`, `open` or `blocked: <reason>`. A receiver treats `open` or `blocked` as a hard gate: it does not pass the point the user named, or take any Consequential action, until the review is met or the user decides. If the field is missing or unclear but the conversation shows a requirement, treat it as `open`. Routine advice does not go here |
| ADVICE | advice obtained in this task that later stages need, newest first; carry prior lines unchanged. For a discussion, list each model with its effort and rounds. {{if .Both}}Diversity is relative to the owner that asked, not the receiver; take the provider from launch metadata. {{end}}Advice is model output, not evidence. Record failed or partial attempts only when they explain a gap or an open REQUIRED REVIEW. No counters, budgets or allowance history; never prompt contents or secrets. `none` if there was no advice |
| DELEGATION STATE | only unresolved activity: children still running or not confirmed stopped, unintegrated or uncleaned task worktrees and branches, blocked cleanup, and kept resumable sessions. Give enough to stop, integrate or recover each one. Omit children that are finished, integrated and cleaned; their results go in CONTEXT or ADVICE, with their source and whether the owner verified them. `none` otherwise |
| WHY | header only: {{if .Both}}distinguish an actual task advantage from comparable choices with the default tie-break (Claude), the declared plan allowance or an observed usage limit, a user-named choice or advisor diversity. A practical tie is sufficient supported fit, not identical scores; a better task fit on GPT beats the Claude default. Explain{{else}}explain{{end}} the selected model and effort. If the declared plan allowance or an observed usage limit changed the order of comparable routes, say which |
| PROMPT | fenced `md`; begins with the exact active-workflow and mandatory-skill lines; contains the receiving stage, exposure/shape, task, REQUIRED REVIEW, ADVICE and DELEGATION STATE without relying on the short header; excludes model recommendations and WHY; self-contained; no `PASTE BACK` |

Every handoff names the next `/handoff` in `EXPECTED OUTPUT` — except Verify Implementation, which offers Plan Next Version instead. A handoff missing that is incomplete.

{{if .Both}}ADVICE labels provider diversity relative to the owner that asked, not the receiver. {{end}}Ordinary sender identity does not belong in the prompt.

Launching a stage owner via {{if .Both}}`codex exec` or `claude -p`{{else if .Claude}}`claude -p`{{else}}`codex exec`{{end}}: pass the entire fenced `PROMPT:`, from `HANDOFF WORKFLOW` through `EXPECTED OUTPUT`. The launched stage owner is an owner, not an advisor or worker. Advisors and workers receive only their compact packet (`delegate`), never the handoff prompt.

## Implement → Verify

The verifier has no shared context — anything omitted is unavailable to it. The prompt must carry:

- what changed, by functional area; under granted OWNERSHIP, every change the task did not ask for
- exact files to inspect
- checks passed / failed / blocked, and why blocked ones are environmental rather than implementation failures
- committed / uncommitted / staged state
- commit hash, or the exact no-commit reason
- residual risks

